New evidence
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bangkok votes in new governor for first time in nine years","publisher":"Euronews / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-05-23","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/05/23/us-thailand-politics-bangkok","claim":"Independent candidate Chadchart Sittipunt won Bangkok's first governor election in nine years by a record 1.3 million votes, in a race with 31 candidates and 4.4 million eligible voters.","locator":"lines 658-666","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters contemporaneous election-result report with direct vote totals and clear context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but relevant: it shows meaningful electoral contestation under Prayut's government, not a personal action by Prayut.","contrary_evidence":["It is a local race, not a national election, so it does not by itself prove national democratic openness."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.3","4B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thai opposition seeks court ruling to halt move to change election system","publisher":"Euronews / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-07-08","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/07/08/us-thailand-politics","claim":"Thailand's biggest opposition party said pro-administration lawmakers were trying to change the electoral system in an unconstitutional way that would favor the government, while Prayut neared the end of his term.","locator":"lines 660-670","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters report on a live parliamentary dispute and opposition allegation, with explicit attribution to pro-administration lawmakers.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect: the report ties the move to pro-administration lawmakers and Prayut's coalition, not to a proven personal order from Prayut.","contrary_evidence":["Government spokesman denied it had anything to do with Prayut or his administration."],"lenses":["4B.2","4B.8","4B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Explainer-How a Thai court suspended Prime Minister Prayuth","publisher":"Euronews / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-08-25","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/08/25/thailand-politics-suspension","claim":"The Constitutional Court suspended Prayuth Chan-ocha from official duties while reviewing whether he had hit the eight-year term limit set in the 2017 constitution written by a military-appointed committee after the coup.","locator":"lines 658-666","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters contemporaneous constitutional-court report with direct legal and political context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Prayut's own tenure and the opposition petition, while also showing inherited post-coup constitutional constraints.","contrary_evidence":["The suspension was temporary and came from the court, not from Prayut's own initiative."],"lenses":["4B.8","4B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Suspended Thai PM Prayuth says to continue as defence minister","publisher":"Reuters via The TBS News","publication_date":"2022-08-26","url":"https://www.tbsnews.net/world/suspended-thai-pm-prayuth-says-continue-defence-minister-484094","claim":"After the court suspension, Prayuth said he would continue his duties as defence minister, while Prawit Wongsuwan served as acting prime minister.","locator":"opening paragraph","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters dispatch with a direct statement from Prayut and a clear caretaker arrangement.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022 and during the suspension window.","ruler_attribution":"Direct: Prayut personally asserted ongoing defence-minister authority despite losing PM duties temporarily.","contrary_evidence":["This does not prove he controlled the premiership during suspension; it shows continued influence through another office."],"lenses":["4B.5","4B.8","4B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Constitutional Court rules Prayut can stay on as PM","publisher":"Nation Thailand","publication_date":"2022-09-30","url":"https://www.nationthailand.com/thailand/politics/40020589","claim":"The Constitutional Court ruled 6:3 that Prayut had not exceeded the eight-year limit because the count began on April 6, 2017, allowing him to continue as prime minister.","locator":"opening summary and Article 158 excerpt","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Local outlet reporting the formal court holding and the date-counting rationale; strong for the legal outcome, though not the court text itself.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Prayut's tenure; this is a successful legal defense of incumbency, not a democratic opening by itself.","contrary_evidence":["It preserves incumbency rather than demonstrating respect for succession limits in a substantive sense."],"lenses":["4B.8","4B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thailand","publisher":"Reuters Institute for the Study of Journalism","publication_date":"2022-06-15","url":"https://reutersinstitute.politics.ox.ac.uk/digital-news-report/2022/thailand","claim":"The report says self-censorship remained widespread in mainstream Thai media, but protest-era digital publishers created an alternative media system that openly criticized the government and the monarchy.","locator":"lines 36-37 of the page","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"University/Reuters Institute country report based on survey and expert analysis; strong for media environment, though not a legal finding.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published in mid-2022 and describing the 2022 information environment.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect: it describes the broader environment under Prayut's government rather than a single decree or personal order.","contrary_evidence":["The same report indicates online and protest media had expanded, so the space was not wholly closed."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thai Journalists Association annual media situational report for 2022","publisher":"Thai Journalists Association","publication_date":"2022-12-28","url":"https://tja.or.th/view/activities/media-movements/1439761","claim":"The association said the culture of self-censorship lived on in 2022, even as Thailand's RSF press-freedom ranking improved to 115, because libel, lese-majeste, and Computer Crimes Act provisions continued to discourage straightforward coverage.","locator":"lines 94-99","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Local professional association assessment with a specific ranking reference and explanation of ongoing legal chill.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"End-of-year 2022 assessment.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but meaningful: it reflects the political environment under Prayut's government, not a single personal order.","contrary_evidence":["The report notes a modest improvement in press-freedom ranking, so the environment was restrictive but not static."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thailand urges care over content as Lazada promotion angers royalists","publisher":"Reuters via VnExpress International","publication_date":"2022-05-07","url":"https://e.vnexpress.net/news/world/thailand-urges-care-over-content-as-lazada-promotion-angers-royalists-4460693.html","claim":"Thailand's government warned creators to be careful with content referencing the monarchy after a promotional video angered royalists, and the spokesman said such content could be against the law.","locator":"opening paragraphs and spokesman warning","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters dispatch with a direct government statement and contemporaneous event details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exactly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct enough to the Prayut administration because the warning came from its government spokesman; not necessarily from Prayut personally.","contrary_evidence":["The incident was about private content and royalist reaction, not a formal censorship order against a named opposition outlet."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.9"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"GeckoSpy: Pegasus Spyware Used Against Thailand's Pro-Democracy Movement","publisher":"The Citizen Lab, University of Toronto","publication_date":"2022-07-17","url":"https://citizenlab.ca/research/geckospy-pegasus-spyware-used-against-thailands-pro-democracy-movement/","claim":"Citizen Lab forensically confirmed at least 30 Pegasus infections targeting Thai pro-democracy protesters, activists, academics, lawyers, and NGO workers, and said the infections occurred between October 2020 and November 2021.","locator":"key findings lines 100-102 and attribution section 369-371","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Technical forensic investigation with corroboration from Thai NGOs and Amnesty Security Lab methodology checks.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"The infections predate 2022 but the report was published in 2022 and describes the surveillance climate that persisted into the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Attribution is limited: the report does not conclusively name the operator, but reasonably infers a government operator because Pegasus is sold to governments.","contrary_evidence":["The report does not prove a direct Prayut order or identify a specific agency; timing is mostly 2020-2021 rather than 2022."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.9","4B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Dissecting Thailand's 2022 Military and Police Reshuffles: Palace Proactivity?","publisher":"ISEAS - Yusof Ishak Institute","publication_date":"2022-09-23","url":"https://www.iseas.edu.sg/articles-commentaries/iseas-perspective/2022-95-dissecting-thailands-2022-military-and-police-reshuffles-palace-proactivity-by-paul-chambers","claim":"The 2022 military and police reshuffles were heavily shaped by political considerations, and Prayut Chan-o-cha and Prawit Wongsuwan appointed their favourites to senior army and police posts while palace influence increased.","locator":"executive summary and introduction / conclusion","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Expert policy analysis from a respected regional research institute; strong on appointments and factionalization, though still interpretive.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022 and specifically about the appointment cycle that year.","ruler_attribution":"Directly relevant: the paper names Prayut and Prawit as appointing favourites, but also notes palace influence, so attribution is mixed and not fully personal.","contrary_evidence":["The report also says palace proactivity increased, so not all influence was Prayut's.","Appointment influence does not automatically prove democratic backsliding in every dimension."],"lenses":["4B.4","4B.5","4B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thai police use rubber bullets to disperse protest over APEC summit","publisher":"Reuters via ThePrint","publication_date":"2022-11-18","url":"https://theprint.in/world/thai-police-use-rubber-bullets-to-disperse-protest-over-apec-summit/1223029/","claim":"Thai police fired rubber bullets to disperse an anti-APEC protest, and Reuters reported that at least 10 protesters were arrested and the protest was aimed at Prayut Chan-o-cha's ouster.","locator":"opening paragraphs of the Reuters copy","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters contemporaneous report with direct event details and protest context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exactly in 2022 and tied to an in-country political protest.","ruler_attribution":"Direct to the Prayut government because the protest targeted him and police action was under his administration; the immediate actors were police commanders, not Prayut personally.","contrary_evidence":["The report also notes protesters clashed with police and that officers were injured, so the event was not one-sided state abuse alone."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.9","4B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thailand: Stop using force to crack down on APEC protests","publisher":"Amnesty International Thailand","publication_date":"2022-11-19","url":"https://www.amnesty.or.th/en/news/2022/11/thailand-stop-using-force-to-crack-down-on-apec-protests/","claim":"Amnesty said the 18 November APEC crackdown caused at least nine injuries among protesters and four reporters and led to 25 arrests, describing the response as violent suppression of peaceful assembly.","locator":"opening paragraph and lines 54-67","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct human-rights monitoring organization with specific event counts and a clear rights-based interpretation, useful as independent corroboration of Reuters.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exactly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Prayut government through the crowd-control police; direct personal orders are not shown, so responsibility is institutional and supervisory rather than purely personal.","contrary_evidence":["Amnesty reports both injuries to protesters and injuries to officers, and the event involved a protest against the summit."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.9","4B.10"]}

Search and rejection summary
- I reviewed roughly 30 candidate documents across Reuters/Euronews, Reuters Institute, Citizen Lab, Amnesty, ISEAS, Thai Journalists Association, Nation, Reuters-syndicated local outlets, and several search-result-only hits.
- Rejected as duplicates or low-yield: ReutersConnect image-only pages, syndication mirrors that added no new factual claim, and 2026 local-election pages that were out of period.
- Rejected as already covered by the local structured prior: Freedom House, V-Dem, RSF, and other structured dataset pages that would have repeated supplied priors rather than add ruler-specific narrative.
- Rejected as too weak or too indirect for final use: older 2020-2021 protest explainers unless they were needed only for retrospective context, and pages that returned cache-miss/internal-error with no usable text.
- Kept only the sources that added non-duplicative ruler-period evidence: election contestability, rule-change disputes, term-limit litigation, media self-censorship, surveillance, security reshuffles, and protest policing.

Remaining gaps
- 4B.4 is still thin on direct proof that Prayut strengthened independent courts, legislatures, audit bodies, or local government; the record mainly shows those bodies constraining him or his coalition politicizing appointments.
- 4B.7 remains weak on political equality for minorities, women, and excluded groups in 2022; I did not find a strong, ruler-specific source that tied those outcomes to Prayut's direct choices.
- 4B.2 would be stronger with direct Thai-language parliamentary or constitutional text on the election-system amendment sponsors, rather than relying on Reuters reporting of an opposition challenge.
- 4B.9 would benefit from a direct official or forensic source naming the operational chain behind surveillance and any 2022 digital-control orders beyond monarchy-sensitive speech warnings and the Pegasus report.
